                "4bbd02dce8fe574ecae5efda089e02940178cac7"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/45/76445/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Thang Tran",
                "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                "username": "thangtran-ampere"
            },
            "createdOn": 1733887713,
            "author": {
                "name": "Thang Tran",
                "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                "username": "thangtran-ampere"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "There's only 2 reference voltages, and they're trivially detectable (we only use 3 models of BMC)  Why would we add values to every sensor on every platform rather than just make the ADC base detected in code?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Thang Tran",
                        "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "thangtran-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "> There's only 2 reference voltages, and they're trivially detectable (we only use 3 models of BMC) \n\nWhen we use the iio-hwmon dirver to detect the ADC sensors, the linux kernel of iio-hwmon does not include the reference voltage values. If we add one more property for ADC to present reference voltage, it is not clear to map reference voltage to Max/MinValue of sensors.\n\n> Why would we add values to every sensor on every platform rather than just make the ADC base detected in code?\n\nSome sensors like FAN/PSU also support configuration Max/MinValue. About others applications, I don't know the current status of them, that why I only add Max/MinValue configuration for ADC and Hwmon-temp in this commit."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> > There's only 2 reference voltages, and they're trivially detectable (we only use 3 models of BMC) \n> \n> When we use the iio-hwmon dirver to detect the ADC sensors, the linux kernel of iio-hwmon does not include the reference voltage values. If we add one more property for ADC to present reference voltage, it is not clear to map reference voltage to Max/MinValue of sensors.\n\nNo, but it makes it clear what the property is used for.  \"min/max\" value would have to be computed by hand otherwise.\n\n> \n> > Why would we add values to every sensor on every platform rather than just make the ADC base detected in code?\n> \n> Some sensors like FAN/PSU also support configuration Max/MinValue. About others applications, I don't know the current status of them, that why I only add Max/MinValue configuration for ADC and Hwmon-temp in this commit.\n\nRight, because those sensors don't support scaling, so they're reading a known value."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Thang Tran",
                        "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "thangtran-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "> No, but it makes it clear what the property is used for.  \"min/max\" value would have to be computed by hand otherwise.\n\nYes, users have to calculate the Max/MinValue. But I think this is more meaningful for users to understand the purpose of thos properties. Beside that, I want to unify the method to configure Max/MinValue properties between sensors type."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Can we do something to make parsing of these properties common, and not spread around the code-base?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Thang Tran",
                        "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "thangtran-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "> Can we do something to make parsing of these properties common, and not spread around the code-base?\n\nThe FAN sensors are getting Max/MinValue from the MaxReading/MinReading properties in the EM configuration, the PSU sensors are getting the Max/MinValue base on the <label>_Max/Min properties. Therefore, we can not have a common solution for this issue."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Thang Tran",
                        "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "thangtran-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "Do you have any comments for this commit?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Christopher Sides",
                        "email": "christopher.sides@hpe.com",
                        "username": "csides-hpe"
                    },
                    "message": "As I understand the code, this fix replaces hardcoded 'min' and 'max' values (voltage for ADCSensor, temp for HwmonTempSensor) with a version of the values that get read in from Entity-Manager configs (if the values are defined for a given record the JSON config).\n\nAs I understand it, before this fix, 'MinValue' and 'MaxValue' have been valid Entity-Manager record properties, but it seems like no daemons were actually reading in the values outside of the external sensors daemon.\n\nThe changes seem to make sense to me."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Thang Tran",
                        "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "thangtran-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "Your understading is correct. I just want to add one more point, some sensors like External/AirTemp are using `Max/MinValue` properties in the Entity-Manager to set the range of sensor's value"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Thang Tran",
                        "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "thangtran-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "Do you have any comments for this commit?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Zhikui Ren",
                        "email": "zhikuiren8@gmail.com",
                        "username": "ZhikuiRen"
                    },
                    "message": "lgtm"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "I believe fixing the EM schemas should come first, then think about adding new properties after that's been done.\n\nOther than that i have no relevant opinion on this change \ud83d\ude0a"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Thang Tran",
                        "email": "thuutran@amperecomputing.com",
                        "username": "thangtran-ampere"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi @ed@tanous.net, I see that @alexander.hansen@9elements.com moved Max/Min Values to the ADC and Temperature configuration space in the Entity-Manager. I think using the current properties (Max/MinValue) in the Entity-Manger is better than defining new \"RefVol\" property in EM."
